  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nMostly clear skies and light winds have led to patchy fog across\nthe region early this morning. At the moment the fog appears to be\nlight and patchy, as evidenced by the METAR observations that go\non a roller coaster for their visibilities. However, there is\nstill the potential for a bank of more persistent dense fog to\ndevelop per some of the hi resolution models. Will handle any fog\nwith subsequent SPS products if necessary.\n\nRain chances over the weekend continue to look rather pessimistic\nas high pressure wins the battle, as it so often does in this \nscenario. Models continue to show light rain splitting off to our \nnorth and south as low pressure ejects into northern Canada, \nleaving PoPs at a meager 15 to 20%. While a sprinkle or two \nwouldn't be out of the question over central or north-central \nWisconsin, expect most of the forecast area to remain dry.\n\nStarting to see similar decreasing trend with rain chances during\nthe middle of next week as abundant low-level dry air remains in\nplace beneath Hudson Bay high. Chances for widespread rainfall\nstill look on track as PWATs approach 90th percentile, though\nensembles begin to flatten out the upper trough as it approaches\nthe Midwest and encounters sturdy omega ridge. Probabilities for \none inch of rainfall are steady from the previous run, around 20 \nto 30%, although much lower than they were a day or two ago. \nAdditionally, better moisture return and forcing look to remain \noff to our south, which would mean more of a glancing blow to our \nsouthern tier of counties. All in all, signs still point toward a \ndecent stratiform rain setup, though excessive rainfall potential \nremains in question due to prolonged duration and current drought \nstatus. Worth mentioning is that predictability remains fairly low\nthis far out in the forecast period, so there is still time for \nthings to change.\n\nThings get interesting late next week as high pressure builds in\nfrom southern Canada while a low pressure system over the \nsouthern Plains approaches from the southwest. There is some \nquestion how far north this low will get, which will impact POPs \nlate next week. However, given the recent trends for the high to \nwin out would nudge towards the drier solution at this point in \nthe forecast.",
